## Part 3 — Blind auditor 1: single contacts

**Brief:** R5, R6, R7, R8, R9, R10, R11, R12, R13, R14, R15, R16, R17, R18, R23, R26 — wording, rarity counts, named rivals, and the Greek, with the instruction that where it reported a phrase identical, verbatim, exact or found only in certain places it must quote each occurrence as the text prints it.

**Outcome of the round:** five rows reframed, four of them because of a rival the proposing run had never tested; eleven rows stood; two outright errors found. Both errors were in the solo dig, and both were mine.

### The main session's gate on this round

Fifteen decisive claims were reproduced independently, all fifteen confirmed. One detail in the auditor's reasoning was wrong and is corrected here: it described 2 Samuel 14:7's שֵׁם ("name") as a ketiv, where the unpointed word in that verse is the verb שׂום ("to put"). The substance of its point is unaffected.

The reproduced items, as prose a reader can check against the text:

- **Deuteronomy 24:19–21 supplies the whole of Ruth 2's gleaning scene in three consecutive verses:** 24:19 has קָצַר ("reap"), קָצִיר ("harvest"), עֹמֶר ("sheaf") and the triad הַגֵּר הַיָּתוֹם וְהָאַלְמָנָה ("the sojourner, the orphan and the widow"); 24:20 has חָבַט ("when you beat your olive tree") with the same triad; 24:21 the vineyard with the triad again. Deuteronomy 24 also ranks 26th of 859 against Ruth 2 at K=5.
- **שָׁלַף ("draw out") stands in 24 verses of the WLC; twenty have a sword as the object; the only two with a sandal are Ruth 4:7 and 4:8** (the remaining two are Ps 129:6 and Job 20:25).
- **חָמוֹת ("mother-in-law") and כַּלָּה ("daughter-in-law") share no verse in Ruth — zero** — so Micah 7:6 is the only verse in the canon that pairs the two words on which the whole book is built.
- **שִׁבְעָה בָנִים ("seven sons") as an ordered phrase returns Job 1:2 alone** on the plain pass; Psalm 17:14 appears only on the skeletal pass and was read and rejected as a spelling false positive.
- **כִּי־פָקַד יְהוָה אֶת as an ordered phrase stands in three verses — Exod 4:31, 1 Sam 2:21, Ruth 1:6** — so the chain the sweep reported as two verses is three.
- **מִבֵּית לֶחֶם יְהוּדָה ("from Bethlehem of Judah") stands in eight verses**, and with לָגוּר ("to sojourn") in exactly two, Judg 17:8 and Ruth 1:1.

**Positive controls.** Every nil and every "only" above was run against a reference known to contain the item before the absence was reported. The corpus load itself was controlled on verse count: a first run returned 13,917 verses instead of 23,213 because the index was globbed one directory level too shallow and silently omitted all twenty-one books of the Nevi'im. Every figure in that run was wrong in the same direction — it would have made this auditor appear mistaken on nearly every count — and the control is what caught it. **A corpus-wide count is not reportable until the verse total has been printed and checked.**

### The verdicts this file can state

| # | Claim | Prior rating | Verdict | New confidence | Decisive evidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R5 | Exod 4:31 at 1:6 — "heard" + "visited" + YHWH | moderate–high | **Confirmed with nuance** | moderate–high | The ordered phrase is in **three** verses, not two: 1 Sam 2:21 joins Exod 4:31 and Ruth 1:6. The contact holds; the count was understated. |
| R6 | The Genesis toledot heading at 4:18 | high on the formula | **Confirmed with nuance** | high on the formula; the exclusivity claim withdrawn | The formula stands. But "the only book outside the Torah to use it" is false: 1 Chr 1:29 אֵלֶּה תֹּלְדוֹתָם is a heading, with eight further occurrences of the noun in Chronicles. And Genesis 2:4 is fully plene, so the plene/defective contrast the sweep drew does not run as stated. Gen 11:27 and 25:19 are the models. |
| R7 | Mic 7:6 and חָמוֹת | high on the count; moderate–high on the inversion | **Confirmed** and sharpened | high | חָמוֹת and כַּלָּה share **zero** verses in Ruth, so Mic 7:6 is the canon's only verse pairing them. This strengthens rather than weakens the reading. |
| R10 | Deut 22:30 [Heb 23:1] / 27:20 as the idiom the text avoids | high on the lexical facts; moderate–high on the avoidance | **Confirmed**, with a reference error corrected | unchanged | גָּלָה with כָּנָף stands in exactly two verses. But the dig cited "Deut 23:1" in Hebrew numbering throughout: WLC Deuteronomy 22 ends at v29, so Hebrew 23:1 is **English 22:30**. Corrected in all eight places, with a versification note added to the front matter. |
